<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>9.9.4: Rewriting Quadratics</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02485</md:content-id>
    <md:title>9.9.4: Rewriting Quadratics</md:title>
    <md:uuid>1128d943-aee1-495b-a21f-2c6b6c41a9e3</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">For questions 1 - 2, use the function \(f(x)=x^2\) that is shifted to create the function \(g(x)\) that has a vertex at \((3, -2)\).</para>
      <!--Q#1-->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00002">Write \(g(x)\) in vertex form.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00003">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00004">\(g(x)=(x-3)^2-2\) The vertex form of a quadratic is \(f(x)=a(x-h)^2+k\). Since there is not a vertical stretch or compression or reflection mentioned, the vertex values are substituted for \((h, k)\). \(g(x)=(x-3)^2+-2\) becomes \(g(x)=(x-3)^2-2\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#2-->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00005">Write \(g(x)\) in standard form.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00006">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00007">Compare your answer: \(g(x)=x^2-6x+7\) \(g(x)=(x-3)^2-2\) \(g(x)=(x-3)(x-3)-2\) \(g(x)=x^2-3x-3x+9-2\) \(g(x)=x^2-6x+7\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
    </section>
  </content>
</document>